WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.900
- He should have let me
bump off Harvey Dent.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.190
We'd all have been better
off. Wouldn't we, Harv?

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.310
- Yeah, you can't defend
your attempted murder

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.150
by saying that everyone
would be better off

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.151
if you actually completed
the murder itself.

00:00:14.151 --> 00:00:17.110
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.530
Today, we're gonna take a
deep dive into my childhood

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.287
with one of my favorite shows of all time,

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.867
"Batman: The Animated Series."

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.460
- You know, as a kid I used
to watch it with my father.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.293
- I'm a real lawyer,

00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:28.660
so let's react to "Batman:
The Animated Series,"

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.043
the "Trial" episode.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.713
(dramatic music)

00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:34.814
♪ Doodele doo doo ♪

00:00:34.814 --> 00:00:37.731
(thunder crashing)

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.480
All right, "Trail."

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.710
No, I'm sorry. "Trial."

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.170
The "Trial" episode.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.160
All right, Paul Dini and Bruce W. Timm,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.040
we'll see if you can write
a good courtroom episode.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.030
- [Judge] Pamela Lillian Isley,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.150
District Attorney Van Dorn has asked

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.200
that the maximum sentence
of life imprisonment

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.680
be imposed upon you.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.440
- Maximum sentence for what?

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.010
In "Batman: The Animated Series,"

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.440
I don't think anyone ever actually dies,

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.910
and I don't think Poison Ivy,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.180
I think this is a Poison Ivy, right?

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.003
So...
- Call me Poison Ivy.

00:01:07.003 --> 00:01:09.240
- What is she getting the maximum sentence

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.010
of life in prison for?

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.970
I mean, I guess maybe attempted murder.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.800
She did try and kill a lot of people.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:15.633
All right. All right.

00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:18.070
So if she was, I don't know

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.100
what the particular
circumstances are here,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.760
but the district attorney has asked

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.510
for the maximum sentence.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.810
I don't know that that fits
with the sentencing guidelines,

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.270
even though this is New York state,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.010
not actually the federal system,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.570
but I'll allow it, I'll allow it.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:33.880
- [Judge] However, as
your apprehension came

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.580
at the hands of the Batman

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.440
and not a recognized agent of the law,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.310
this court has no choice.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.700
- Okay. All right.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.920
A couple of big problems here.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.710
First of all, we've talked
a lot on this channel

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.430
about how Batman is a vigilante,

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.810
the things he's doing are illegal,

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.490
and subjects him to all kinds
of actual criminal penalties.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.210
Putting that aside, when
Batman apprehends a wrongdoer

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.230
who has also done something wrong,

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.220
that doesn't mean that
they don't go to jail.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.430
They can be sentenced to
jail just like anyone else.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.820
The fact that a vigilante
has captured them,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.560
I don't think has any actual repercussions

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.190
in the actual police and
judicial system itself.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:14.649
- [Judge] This court has no choice

00:02:14.649 --> 00:02:17.720
but to return you to Arkham Asylum,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.410
where it is hoped you will
complete your rehabilitation.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.360
- Okay, but what does that
have to do with anything?

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.040
So if they're captured by Batman,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.310
going to Arkham Asylum
is effectively the result

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.270
of being found guilty of a crime.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.940
Now, Arkham Asylum, my recollection

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.320
is that it's a mental institution,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.100
and if you're found not
guilty by reason of insanity,

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.860
which a lot of these, I
guess, supervillains are,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.500
you could be sent to some
sort of facility like that,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.250
but it's for an indefinite amount of time.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.060
But what the judge is saying
is a non-sequitur here.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.880
He's saying we can't do anything with you

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.440
because you were captured by Batman,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.536
but also I'm gonna send you
to this mental institution

00:02:54.536 --> 00:02:58.012
because you're not guilty
by mental insanity.

00:02:58.012 --> 00:02:59.640
I don't know what's going here.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.169
So far, this doesn't check out.

00:03:01.169 --> 00:03:03.372
(keys clinking)

00:03:03.372 --> 00:03:06.122
(dramatic music)

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:10.426
(door clicking)

00:03:10.426 --> 00:03:12.730
You know, for a mental institute,

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:15.001
this looks a lot like a prison.

00:03:15.001 --> 00:03:17.310
I think they're blurring some lines here

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.330
between a mental institution

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.540
and just a flat-out supermax prison here.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:23.660
- Well, cheer up!

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.663
Word is, we're gonna throw a little party!

00:03:26.663 --> 00:03:29.410
And old Jervis here has the invitations!

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.850
- I mean, I suppose it does make sense

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.890
that the Mad Hatter does
reside in Arkham Asylum,

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.020
more so than anyone else.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.130
I have no reason to believe

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.830
why all of those other criminals
should be in Arkham Asylum

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.970
instead of, you know, an actual prison.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.770
- Not only does Batman create

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.830
these so-called super criminals,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:47.940
he takes it upon himself
to be their judge and jury

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.310
with no regard for the legal system.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.540
- Well, that's not true.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.830
Batman literally leaves the
prisoners on the doorstep

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.420
of the police so that the
police can arrest the prisoners,

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.130
and then theoretically,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.380
they'll be tried by the district attorney,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.803
who once was Harvey Dent,
and now is Miss Van Dorn.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.370
Yeah, I don't think I agree with that.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.750
I think that's unnecessarily cynical.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.630
- [Reporter] It sounds like
you wan to put Batman on trial.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.080
- Believe me, I'd like nothing better.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.240
- Well, yeah, so look,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.400
Batman deserves to be on trial
for a whole host of reasons

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.950
that are apart from the fact

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.670
that he's judge, jury, and executioner.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.540
He's not really any of those things.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.540
He is a vigilante, and
should be put on trial,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.170
but that's sort of neither here nor there.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.750
- As Gotham's new district attorney,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.260
I can't allow your pet nut
to run loose in my city.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.270
- Yeah, that's fair.
- I mean it!

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.000
- Janet, it's a war zone out there,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.760
and Batman is our best weapon.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.670
- That's also fair.
- And I say, he's a drug

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.940
the city keeps taking
to avoid facing reality.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.360
Speak of the devil.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.780
- The East Side Skulls
are ready to declare war

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.000
on the other gangs.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.360
- Old news, my department's
already planning a sting

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.000
to catch the Skulls' leader.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.014
- Don't bother.
(blinds rattling)

00:05:00.014 --> 00:05:01.730
(Devin laughs)
- Very cute, mister.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:05.130
- But so, I mean, the issue
with gangland violence

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.760
is not that you don't know
who the gang members are

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.833
and just you can go arrest them,

00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:12.200
it's that you have to
actually have enough evidence

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.520
that they have done the things
that they were accused of.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.990
And so here, Batman hasn't
done really much good

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.610
because he's just simply captured someone

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.070
that I'm sure the district
attorney and the police

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:25.580
already knew was a member
of this gang system.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.260
So yeah, Batman not doing his best here.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.580
- You want to support law and order,

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.920
you take off that mask
and put on a uniform.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.590
- Yeah. I mean, that's fair.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:38.130
- Next time I see him, it'll
be a whole different story.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.475
- Hmmm. Will it, though?

00:05:40.475 --> 00:05:41.943
- Hello, Bruce.

00:05:41.943 --> 00:05:44.190
- Of course.
- Janet, I'm so glad

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.040
we're able to meet on such short notice.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.560
Still, even you have to
admit Batman's come through

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.393
now and then.

00:05:49.393 --> 00:05:51.570
- But look at the lunatic
fringe he's created,

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.360
the Joker, Two-Face,
Poison Ivy, and the rest,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.510
Batman's responsible
for every one of them.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.390
- Is he? So, okay.

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.040
Is that where this episode is going,

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.950
that Batman is actually responsible

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:07.690
for creating the supervillains
that he is policing as well?

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.940
I mean, ooh. Interesting.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:11.070
Well, we'll see where that goes.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.420
I mean, that might get into
issues of proximate cause

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.725
versus actual cause, but
that's kind of a straw man,

00:06:15.725 --> 00:06:18.980
so it'll be interesting to
see what she does with that.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.083
- I'm sorry, Bruce. I won't be a minute.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:25.850
- Take your time.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.230
- Is that a "I hate to see you go,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.230
but I love watching you leave" joke?

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.520
Did they shoehorn that
into a kids' cartoon here?

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.740
Hmm, I wonder if they're
being a little cheeky there.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.172
No pun intended.

00:06:41.172 --> 00:06:44.010
- [Janet] Let me out! Do you hear me?

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.540
Whoever you are, let me out this second!

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.910
- Again, this really, really
looks like a prison here

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:52.280
rather than any kind of institute

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.660
that would actually be for psychological

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.210
or psychiatric purposes.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.600
- We got some legal
business to settle first.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:00.900
- What do you mean?

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.503
- I'm talking about a trial.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:08.430
Me the prosecutor, you the defense,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.730
and your client... (snaps fingers)

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.380
Is Batman.
- Okay, you know what?

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.798
I like the idea, I like
the setup of this episode,

00:07:17.798 --> 00:07:21.270
making the single-minded district attorney

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.470
who actually doesn't
like Batman to be forced

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:25.760
to defend him.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.940
Though it's interesting that
when these supervillains

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:32.370
finally have the upper hand,
they have Batman incapacitated,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.920
they decide to do this legal nonsense

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.380
instead of, you know,
actually unmasking him

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:37.900
and, you know, killing him.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.790
Yeah, not a great plan
from their perspective,

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.080
but a great conceit for an episode.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:42.913
I love it.

00:07:42.913 --> 00:07:45.332
- Far as I'm concerned, you
belong in here with them!

00:07:45.332 --> 00:07:47.892
(Devon groans)
- (groans) Whatever.

00:07:47.892 --> 00:07:49.560
You've gotta go through with it.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.730
It's their game and their rules.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.020
- You've said it yourself, lady.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.990
Batman made every one of us.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:00.290
- So we are placing him before
the bar to face our justice.

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:02.370
- Okay.
- Amusing idea, what,

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.140
kidnapping you to be Batman's attorney?

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.830
- Okay, so legally speaking,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.990
putting aside the fact that
this trial is completely rigged

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.880
and impossible, this would
be a very, very easy case

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.230
for this lawyer to win

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:20.360
because just because
someone is an actual cause

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.230
doesn't mean that they
are the proximate cause,

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.810
the legal cause of something happening,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.100
and when you're talking about
a bunch of supervillains

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:31.700
who are, I use this term loosely,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.310
in control of their faculties,

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.070
they're committing a
bunch of intentional torts

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.010
and a bunch of intentional crimes.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:41.060
So that Batman, though he
might have placed a domino

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:43.560
or flipped a domino over in
this long string of dominoes

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.220
that led to the creation
of these supervillains,

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.370
he's not actually
responsible for the things

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.203
that they have done.

00:08:49.203 --> 00:08:52.050
So theoretically, this lawyer should have

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:52.957
a pretty easy time.

00:08:52.957 --> 00:08:57.790
- [The Ventriloquist] Likewise
our fair and impartial jury.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.278
- Hang him!
- Shoot him!

00:08:59.278 --> 00:09:00.800
- Hit him with a rock!

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.650
- Okay, so I probably
don't need to tell you

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:07.790
that this jury is completely
constitutionally infirm.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:09.910
First of all, you don't have 12 people,

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:12.250
which is not requirement in all states,

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:13.992
but generally you do have 12.

00:09:13.992 --> 00:09:18.470
(laughs) This is not an
impartial selection of people

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:19.720
in this area.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:23.070
Batman is not being tried
by a selection of his peers.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.240
Under the Sixth Amendment,

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.600
a jury has to be a
representative cross sample

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:29.260
of the people in that
geographical location,

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.140
and not only is this petit jury,

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.950
the I think six
supervillains who are there,

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.230
not representative of
the community at large,

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.320
there was no jury panel,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.400
there was no veneer through
which you could choose

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.700
the members of the jury.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.120
It has to be impartial.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.950
And it doesn't mean that
they can't know anything

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:48.190
about the accused,

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:49.790
but it does mean that they have to be able

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.700
to put what they know aside.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:55.180
And here they are material witnesses,

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.550
they are absolutely not
impartial against Batman,

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.930
and so yeah. (laughs)

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.763
Believe it or not,

00:10:01.763 --> 00:10:04.510
a jury made up exclusively
of supervillains

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.920
does not pass constitutional muster.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.533
- Judge Joker!
(crowd cheering)

00:10:14.585 --> 00:10:15.680
(gavel bangs)
- Guilty!

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.440
- Yeah, so Joker as the judge,

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:21.280
that doesn't pass
constitutional muster either.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.190
He needs to be bounced
for not being capable

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.710
of presiding over this trial.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:30.520
- I was promised a chance
to defend my client.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.703
- Oh, very well.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:34.790
Like it'll make a difference.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:38.680
Mr. Prosecutor, make
your opening statement.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:39.920
- Yeah, so in California,

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:43.610
you have a procedure through
which you ask the judge

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.680
to no longer preside over your trial,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.280
and sometimes you can
just do it automatically.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.830
It's called 170.6, and the judge,

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.680
after that particular section
of the California Code,

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.106
you definitely want to make a 170.6 motion

00:10:54.106 --> 00:10:55.560
in this particular case.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.270
- We're all freaks and monsters,

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.520
and who made us this way?

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:04.319
Batman!
(all shouting angrily)

00:11:04.319 --> 00:11:05.900
- Yeah, not so much.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:06.920
So in law school,

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.410
you spend a huge amount of time

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:10.080
talking about the difference
between actual cause

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.069
and proximate cause, and in fact,

00:11:12.069 --> 00:11:15.270
often you're dealing
with really dry cases.

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:17.360
There is one particular case in law school

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:18.890
that I think is actually
really interesting

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:20.670
that talks about this very distinction.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.260
It's called the Palsgraf case,

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.850
and it involves a guy
who brings some fireworks

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.720
onto a train, the box
of fireworks explodes,

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.170
and it tips over this giant
scale onto this poor woman

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.530
named Palsgraf, and so the
main question in that case

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:35.310
is not whether the guy
who brought the fireworks,

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:37.430
is he responsible, but the question is,

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:40.120
is the railroad at any point responsible?

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.110
And I actually did an entire video

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:43.300
called "What's in the Box?"

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:44.590
that talks about this very case.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:46.220
It's all animated, which is fitting,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.930
'cause this is "Batman:
The Animated Series."

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:50.550
So if you are curious about
how that case turned out

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:53.490
and the difference between
actual and proximate cause,

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:54.750
you can check out that video.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.170
- Yo, Hat! On the stand!

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.670
